    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In order to make college graduates more employable, the national and state governing bodies in India have made considerable efforts at increasing skills training, regulating privatization, and encouraging corporate investments in the nation’s higher education institutions. By attempting to prioritize the job market, however, attention is often deterred from the local access and quality needs of educators and students. My work adds to existing literature and asks: what is the relationship between the top-down priorities of government and the bottom-up needs of employers, students, and educators? I apply stakeholder perspectives to four national and state government policy documents and five local interviews in order to assess where stakeholders align and propose a solution for where they differ in their higher education agendas. The results of my research may be used to guide the Indian government to take a less distant approach when evaluating current education reform needs and setting future skills-based learning priorities.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
